DESIGN.md:316:   - **Preview tag next to an amber note (decision).** The Preview tag stays Amber Notice (it discloses local-only, non-durable data, the amber meaning). With the sync failure now coral, the only amber account note is `Sign-in expired`. Both always carry words and never touch in practice: the tag exists only in the browser preview, and the account notes only matter in the native app. No change.
DESIGN.md:319:   - **Provider mail drawer.** Classes in the shared stylesheet instead of scoped bare element rules: one Ash intro line (`Provider snapshots.`, the detail as title and hidden text), a Seam-underline search, ghost buttons (`.drawer-ghost`), compact ~28px ghost rows with no card or border, plain errors through the shared sentences (`Could not load destinations. The network is unavailable. Showing the previous snapshot.`), a static loading line where the list will appear, and an empty-state line. Without an account the drawer's note (`Provider mail needs a connected Gmail account.`) carries the way forward, read-only: `Connect Gmail` when disconnected, `Account` when not configured (nothing else; it uses the same connect path as the empty inbox line, and the drawer keeps focus). The intro no longer says "Read-only" (the drawer title already does). Rows are real buttons with a roving tab stop: ArrowDown/ArrowUp and J/K move between rows (the search field only takes ArrowDown, so typing is untouched), Enter opens, and Escape steps back reader, page list, catalog, then closes the drawer. Modifier and IME guards, the Cmd+K passthrough and the stale-response guards are unchanged.
DESIGN.md:461:- **Thread above the card:** the card shrinks the thread scroller, so the reader scrolls the message being answered (the latest) fully into view when a reply opens and keeps it there as the card grows (Cc / Subject), by scrolling only; focus never moves. It stops following as soon as the user scrolls (wheel, touch, click) or moves through the thread with J/K or the arrows. A latest message taller than the strip is aligned by its top. Measured (fixture, latest message 166px): the thread strip was 301px with Cc / Subject closed and 241px open at 1280×800, and the latest message showed 162.5px then 102.5px of its 166px (hidden behind the card); now 166px of 166px in both states (scrollTop 9.5 and 69.5), at 1000×700 (strip 201 and 191px) too, and at a 20px root (strip 225.6px, message 207.3px fully visible). The 60% cap is kept and no extra minimum was needed: it leaves at least 40% of the pane.
ARCHITECTURE.md:37:| Android, multi-account, plugins, AI/automation | Out of scope for first build at proposal time (ADR 0001) |
ARCHITECTURE.md:82:4. **SQLite is Rust-only.** The frontend never touches the database directly; there is no SQL
PRODUCT.md:53:Not a formal priority at this stage; no WCAG conformance target or certification is implied. Keyboard operability and visible focus remain required because they are core to the keyboard-first model. Don't regress behavior that already exists (explicit action labels, editable/IME shortcut protection, reduced-motion alternatives, selection/outcome announcements). The existing desktop/contact breakpoints remain authoritative; no new mobile UI. Revisit if the audience expands beyond the author.
/* Preview tag: the short word, then the detail. */
.preview-detail {
  white-space: pre;
}

/* At 1100px and below (DESIGN.md gap 18) the trail yields in this order: the Preview tag first (its detail
   goes, then the whole tag drops to its accessible text while an account note or the read-only hint needs
   the room), then the read-only words (they collapse to the Review access link, still in the accessibility
   tree and the hover title), and last the account note's detail words. The link and the lead never clip. */
@media (max-width: 1100px) {
  .preview-tag {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .preview-detail {
    display: none;
  }

  .app-header:has(.account-status-note:not(.visually-hidden), .access-hint) .preview-tag {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
  grid-row: 3;
}

.app-main > .contact-column {
  grid-column: 3;
  grid-row: 1 / -1;
}

@media (min-width: 1000px) {
  .app-main {
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr) var(--contact-width);
  }

  /* No rows, no conversation, no contact: the column is not rendered and its track goes, so the list takes the width. */
  .app-main.contact-collapsed {
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
  }
}

